    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 56 tests pass with a clean build. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `timezone` field on the owner response, derived from the owner's locality/region via the fixed region-to-timezone table (NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney, VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne, QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane), returned as an IANA name.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `timezone` string field to the Owner schema (right after `locality`), so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/Localities.java`** \u2014 Added a fixed `REGION_TIMEZONE` table and a `timezone(Owner owner)` helper that reuses the existing `region(Owner)` derivation and maps it to the IANA name (null when the region has no known timezone, e.g. `UNKNOWN`).\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a MapStruct `@Mapping` for `timezone` that calls `Localities.timezone(owner)`, mirroring the existing `locality` mapping.\n\nThe new `Cp42Tests` (asserting `locality` = `NSW` and `timezone` = `Australia/Sydney` for a Sydney owner) passes, and the full suite is green (56 tests, 0 failures). The derivation is layered on the existing region logic, so an owner's timezone stays consistent with its locality (including the postcode/customerCode-based region resolution).",
